{"kind":"report","text":"CHANGES REQUESTED on exact clean pushed head 3d3806faabcac9aa31f6c11bfad4c9f9b87c90be, artifact 598cad0a at cmd/gs — narrowly, and on nothing about the behaviour. The change is correct and I found no correctness or security defect in it. Two cheap items and one request I am not blocking on.\n\nWHAT I VERIFIED RATHER THAN READ. All five by-name guards were removed one at a time, each confirmed applied and compiling, and each named test failed: the resident-URL lookup kills TestPublishedResidentAnswersWithoutServerFlag, the status frontier validation kills TestAdvertisedResidentWithDivergentFrontierIsRefused, the no-advertisement fallback kills TestNoAdvertisedResidentFallsBackLocally, the dash case kills TestServerDashForcesTheLocalFold, and the query-path guard at query.go:49 kills TestAskResidentRefusesDivergentWorkQueryFrontier. That last one is the guard I proved uncovered on your earlier head by deleting it and watching cmd/gs stay green; it is genuinely covered now.\n\nThe dash escape hatch works on all four query commands after the rebase — I built gs, ran a real resident, and read the source label each command prints: work, artifacts, inspect and reviews each say resident by default and verified local under --server -. I also killed the resident with SIGKILL so the advertisement survived: reads fall back loudly on all five surfaces, gs state refuses with rc=1, and --server - still succeeds. A SIGTERM withdraws the advertisement cleanly and a foreign-genesis record is ignored. All three of my earlier findings are answered: openForQuery is gone from the tree entirely, statusCommands duplicate validation is deleted, and validateLoopbackServer returns the trimmed URL with both callers using the returned value.\n\nARCHITECTURE. The affected layers are 3, nexus and live runtime, and 7, the CLI. The head changes the layer 3 Advertisement contract — a client meeting a withdrawn or stale address no longer simply acts locally, because durable writes now refuse — and architecture.md:210-216 carries that change in the same head, which is right. Layer 7 needed no edit. The twelve gs reference pages match resolveServerURL exactly; I read every changed row, and serve.mds two named limits are both true rather than hedges.\n\nFIRST ITEM. The page is changed but no artifact stands at it. docs/reference/architecture.md has eight artifacts and none live — I checked the projection rather than inferring. AGENTS.md step 4 requires the contract change to update the page in the same head and publish its candidate artifact there; the second clause is unmet, and until it is, nothing can ever flare that page when the behaviour moves under it. If you judge the section 3 edit a clarification rather than a contract change, this ground disappears and only the second item stands. I would rather you made that call than have me impose mine.\n\nSECOND ITEM. validateLoopbackServer is now a one-line passthrough to residentclient.ValidateURL with two call sites, both inside resolveServerURL. I read it at your head to be sure: the body is a single return. It adapted a signature before this head and adapts nothing now. Deleting it also removes TestValidateLoopbackServer, which duplicates TestValidateURL table for table. And the case \"\" arms bind-check-return is exactly the one-line return the default arm below it already uses. Together that is nine lines for resolveServerURL, one fewer function and one fewer duplicate test, with nothing weakened. AGENTS.md makes cutting fluff a review duty, which is the only reason this is a change request rather than a remark.\n\nNOT BLOCKING, BUT WORTH DOING. A resident.json holding a non-loopback URL makes every gs command refuse, which is the right call, but the message names no way out and --server - is the only one. ResidentHeldError in internal/app/resident.go already carries a Recovery field with the exact command; matching it is one line at main.go:1630.\n\nOUT OF SCOPE, WORTH ITS OWN REQUEST. actorAddCommand, actorRetireCommand, roleGrantCommand and roleRevokeCommand are durable writers with no --server flag at all, so they always write locally and now race a resident everything else defaults to. cmd/gitseq-github takes an explicit --server with no default. Both predate this head and neither is a regression here.\n\nGATES I RAN AT THIS EXACT HEAD. gofmt -l . empty, git diff --check clean, go vet ./... clean, go test ./cmd/gs/... -count=1 ok at 165s. Because the head edits fourteen documentation pages I also ran go test ./internal/docset/... -count=1, which is ok at 85s.\n\nFOR THE MERGE, WHENEVER IT COMES. cmd/gs currently holds two live artifacts, yours at 3d3806fa and mine at f4281e80 from the fast-test lane, and neither of us can retire the others. Planners proposal 545dfb2f would not cover this pair, because mine is not abandoned. Worth settling before the merge rather than after; I have filed three cleanup requests to Hugh today for exactly this shape.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:75f4cb7da9bc60ab1012dbd635b4daaf598cad0a","head":"3d3806faabcac9aa31f6c11bfad4c9f9b87c90be","stale":"true","staleness":"promise, request stale; retired bases: git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:02ce62ac39d438188a2ab6ac783b4edf22d7bd5e","verdict":"changes-requested"}}